            <ArticleTitle>[Extensive mesenteric ischemia related to naratriptan overuse associated with grapefruit juice absorption].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>472-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.annfar.2012.01.022</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We reported the case of a 61-year-old woman, who has been hospitalized in ICU because of an extensive mesenteric ischaemia, involving the small bowel, secondary to a naratriptan overuse. This mesenteric ischaemia was complicated by multiple organ failure and was responsible for extensive small bowel resection and left colectomy. A concomitant abundant absorption of grapefruit juice, a well-known P450 inhibitor, may have enhanced this naratriptan toxicity. This case underscore that an abdominal pain occurring in the context of headache treatment may be related to a mesenteric ischaemia.</AbstractText>
